// SECURITY -- CLOSED. This was the last narrowed residual of the closure/
// fn-value laundering fix: a closure/fn-value flowing through a
// parameter/local/return/passthrough-chain/actor-message/spawn/generic/dyn
// shape was closed first (see CLOSED table in `tools/loop/LEDGER.md`); THIS
// shape -- a closure stored in a CONTAINER (struct field / array element /
// map value / nested combination) and read back out and invoked by code
// that never held the capability it carries -- is closed by this session's
// fix to `hot_params`/`accumulate_hot_extra_caps` in
// `kryos-capabilities/src/checker.rs`.
//
// Root cause (as found): `hot_params`'s seed pass only marked a PARAMETER
// hot when its OWN declared type was `fn(...) -> ...`; a parameter typed
// `Registry` (a struct with a function-typed FIELD), `[fn() -> str]`, or
// `map<str, fn() -> str>` never matched, so `zero_cap_tool`'s param `reg`
// was never marked hot, and `resolve_closure_caps` never got a chance to
// trace `reg.reader`/`arr[i]`/`m[k]` back to the value written into it.
//
// Fix: `is_fn_bearing_type` recognizes a struct with >=1 function-typed
// field, an array whose element type is a function, and a `map<K, V>` whose
// VALUE type is a function (recursively, so a struct field holding an array
// of closures also qualifies). `hot_params`'s seed pass now ALSO detects
// `obj.field(...)` / `arr[i](...)` / `m[k](...)` invocation shapes (a
// `PathStep` chain from the parameter to the invoked slot), validated
// against the parameter's declared type (`resolve_type_path`) so an
// ordinary method call sharing a field's name is never misclassified.
// `resolve_container_path_caps` traces a struct/array/map LITERAL's
// construction site down that same path (union every value written into an
// array/map element -- index-insensitive, conservative; a struct field is
// traced precisely by name), falling back to `Unknown` -> `Capability::All`
// when the container is built from a non-literal source (a `push`ed loop, a
// function return, a read from another container) -- the same sound
// fallback already used for every other unresolvable shape.
//
// See `cap_escape_closure_launder_array.kry`, `..._map.kry`, and
// `..._nested.kry` for the array-element, map-value, and
// struct-field-holding-an-array-of-closures siblings of this repro. All four
// are gated in `tests/security_gate.sh` (checks #7-10).
//
// ACCEPTANCE (met): this program is REJECTED (E0507) under both
// `--capabilities-mode=inferred` (default) and `--strict-capabilities`.
// Verified BOTH ways: reverting the fix and rebuilding makes this compile
// clean and print the secret again (proving the test is not vacuous).

struct Registry { reader: fn() -> str }

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

fn zero_cap_tool(reg: Registry) -> str {
    return reg.reader()
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let r = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let reg = Registry { reader: r }
    deny!(fs:read) {
        // Pre-fix: compiled clean, ran, and printed the secret.
        println("struct-field launder (was NOT CLOSED): " + zero_cap_tool(reg))
    }
}
